<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sarah Forbes Bonetta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1843–<a href="/years/1880">1880</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Aina, Ina, Sally Forbes Bonetta</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sarah Forbes Bonetta, born Aina, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> girl enslaved in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> who became the ward of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>. In 1848 the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> captured her village, Oke-Odan, and killed her parents, and she spent two years as a child slave at King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghezo">Gezo</a>'s court. In 1850 Gezo gave her as a diplomatic gift to Captain Frederick E. Forbes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>, who had failed to persuade him to end the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>; Forbes named her after himself and his ship and presented her to the Queen, who paid for her education in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. In 1862 she married the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> merchant James Pinson Labulo Davies. She died of tuberculosis at Funchal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madeira">Madeira</a>, in 1880.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sarah-forbes-bonetta.jpg" alt="Sarah Forbes Bonetta" width="330" height="309" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Camille Silvy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sara_Forbes_Bonetta_(15_September_1862)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Enslaved women were sold with men and children, and in 1849 Ghezo gave a captive Yoruba girl, later named <b>Sarah Forbes Bonetta</b>, to the British naval officer Frederick Forbes as a “gift”.</blockquote><a href="/societies/dahomey">Social Hierarchy of the Kingdom of Dahomey</a></li></ul></section>
